[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: csw1, csw2
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Core switching plane – Linux bridge br0 interacting with the LACP core peer link (bond0) and its member interfaces – causing STP churn and transient L3 adjacency loss (OSPF).

Mechanism: Both cores are running a Linux bridge (br0). The bridge contains the LACP master (bond0) and the individual member links (eth1, eth2), and even routed ports (e.g., eth0). This creates duplicate Layer‑2 paths and continuous STP topology changes. When STP ports on br0 oscillate between states, the FDB is flushed and traffic momentarily blackholes. Simultaneously, FRR OSPF neighbors drop and reform, producing the “core adjacencies flapping” symptom and user-visible freezes.

Localization evidence:
- On csw1 and csw2 dmesg shows br0 repeatedly transitioning ports: “br0: port 2(eth1) … port 3(eth2) … port 4(bond0) entered forwarding state … topology change detected, sending tcn bpdu” and “br0: port 1(eth0) entered forwarding state,” confirming br0 contains bond0 plus eth1/eth2 (and eth0).
- FRR OSPF neighbors on both cores have very short uptimes (≈50–90 seconds) across all adjacencies, indicating a recent fabric-wide flap.
- FRR logs show bond0 speed changes (aggregator transitions), which, combined with the bridge miswiring, amplifies STP TCNs.

Root cause: Misconfiguration on csw1 and csw2 where the Linux bridge br0 includes both the LACP master (bond0) and its slave interfaces (eth1, eth2), and even routed ports, creating L2 loops and STP TCN storms.

Corrective fix: On csw1 and csw2, remove eth1, eth2, and any routed ports (e.g., eth0/eth3–eth6) from br0. If a bridge is required, only the correct L2-facing interface(s) should be members; never add both bond0 and its slaves. Preferably eliminate br0 on the cores and keep all core links pure L3. After change, verify no br0 TCNs in dmesg and that OSPF neighbor uptimes remain stable. 
=== END ===